- # User Mirror — Cognitive Function Portraits for Validation
- When the user shares their type (either as function stack like "Ti-Ne" or MBTI shorthand
- like "INTP"), respond with a brief, warm "I see you" mirror before moving on. Keep it 3-5
- sentences. Tone: like a friend who gets you, not a textbook.
- Each portrait is organized by dominant function. The four-letter shorthand is included for
- familiarity.
- ## Portraits (workplace-focused, conversational tone)
- You're the one who actually thinks about why something works, not just whether it works.
- In conversations you sometimes get lost building the perfect mental model while the other
- person just wanted a yes or no. Your strength is seeing angles nobody else considered;
- your trap is over-explaining your reasoning when the audience just needs the conclusion.
- You value intellectual honesty and get quietly frustrated when people argue from authority
- instead of logic.
- You're the troubleshooter who stays calm when everything's on fire. You communicate
- sparingly — every word earns its place. Your superpower is practical problem-solving under
- pressure; your challenge is that people sometimes read your quietness as disengagement or
- not caring. You respect competence and autonomy, and micromanagement is your kryptonite.
- You're the person who walks into a room and the meeting suddenly has structure. You think
- in outcomes and timelines, and you genuinely don't understand why others can't just... do
- the thing. Your directness is a superpower that occasionally lands as steamrolling. You
- respect people who push back with good arguments — it's the ones who fold or get emotional
- that frustrate you.
- You're the execution engine — once a decision is made, you make it happen and expect others
- to keep up. You communicate directly and with structure (agenda, bullet points, action
- items). Your superpower is turning chaos into process; your challenge is that "just trust
- the process" doesn't work on everyone. You respect hierarchy and accountability — people
- who dodge responsibility genuinely confuse you.
- You're the person who quietly evaluates everything against an internal compass that you
- can't always articulate but absolutely feel. You communicate authentically — corporate
- fakeness makes your skin crawl. Your strength is seeing the human dimension others miss;
- your challenge is that negative feedback (giving or receiving) can feel physically
- uncomfortable. You do your best work when you believe in what you're doing.
- You're the person who brings taste, authenticity, and quiet conviction to everything you
- do. You communicate through action more than words — you'd rather show than tell. Your
- strength is doing quality work that speaks for itself; your challenge is that in workplaces
- that reward self-promotion, your work can go unseen. You need autonomy and alignment with
- your values to stay engaged.
- You're the natural connector — the person who makes everyone feel heard in a meeting and
- somehow also drives outcomes. You communicate warmly and persuasively, sometimes to the
- point where people don't realize you're steering. Your blind spot is over-indexing on
- harmony — sometimes the conflict IS the necessary conversation. You energize groups but
- occasionally forget to check your own battery.
- You're the team glue — the person who knows everyone's birthday, senses tension in a Slack
- message, and creates the conditions for collaboration. You communicate warmly and
- inclusively, and you genuinely care that everyone's okay. Your strength is social
- infrastructure that others take for granted; your trap is taking criticism of the group
- personally. You work best when the vibe is right.
- You're the person with a plan three steps ahead that you haven't told anyone about yet.
- You communicate efficiently — maybe too efficiently for some people who need more warmth
- wrapping. Your natural move is to optimize the system; your blind spot is sometimes
- forgetting the system has feelings. You respect competence above all, and you can smell
- bullshit from a mile away.
- You're the one who reads the room before anyone else does, often knowing what someone needs
- before they say it. You communicate with layers — there's always more under what you
- actually say. Your superpower is long-game people strategy; your trap is holding things in
- until you suddenly hit a wall. You want depth in work relationships, not just transactional
- exchanges.
- You're the idea machine who lights up a room with possibilities — and sometimes exhausts
- it too. You argue for sport, which you find fun and others sometimes find threatening.
- Your gift is reframing problems in ways that unlock new solutions; your challenge is that
- you start 10 things and finish 3. You need intellectual sparring partners, not yes-people.
- You're the enthusiasm engine who makes people believe things are possible. You communicate
- with energy and story — data alone bores you, but data wrapped in a narrative? You're in.
- Your gift is making connections between ideas nobody else sees; your trap is that your
- excitement can accidentally minimize others' legitimate concerns. You need variety and
- meaning — routine without purpose kills you slowly.
- You're the person everyone actually depends on, even if they don't say it. You communicate
- clearly, factually, and with preparation — you did the homework and it shows. Your
- superpower is reliability that compounds over time; your challenge is that when the plan
- changes suddenly, you need a beat to recalibrate. You respect people who follow through on
- what they said they'd do. It's that simple.
- You're the person who remembers everyone's preferences, holds institutional memory, and
- keeps the machine running smoothly. You communicate considerately — always weighing how
- something will land. Your strength is creating stability others can build on; your trap is
- absorbing too much responsibility because saying no feels selfish. You notice when things
- are off before anyone else does.
- You're the person who makes things happen by reading the room in real-time and adapting on
- the fly. You communicate with energy and directness — no fluff, let's go. Your superpower
- is tactical brilliance in the moment; your challenge is that long-term planning feels like
- being trapped. You respect people who can keep up, and you lose patience with
- over-thinkers who never ship.
- You're the energy in the room — the person who makes work feel less like work. You
- communicate with warmth, humor, and directness. Your superpower is creating buy-in through
- enthusiasm and making people feel good about what they're doing; your challenge is that
- heavy structure and long-horizon planning drain you. You need to see real-world impact to
- stay motivated.
